module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.summary_counters_aggregate_over_a_disposition_set_the_verdict_is_not_in

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data summary_counters_aggregate_over_a_disposition_set_the_verdict_is_not_in: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "summary_counters_aggregate_over_a_disposition_set_the_verdict_is_not_in" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a refusal summary whose every counter reads clean, because the counters aggregate over a disposition set that does not contain the one that fired** (INVALID STATE: a run prints a terminal verdict beside a fixed set of counters, and the disposition that actually caused the refusal is not among them. Every number reads zero and the verdict reads REFUSED. The reader is handed a summary that is TRUE IN EVERY FIELD and answers a question adjacent to the one they asked, and the cause is on a different line that the summary does not point at. HARM, AND IT IS WORSE THAN FAILING TO EXPLAIN: the shape FORECLOSES the question. A refusal with four zeros beside it reads as a clean run that refused for no stated reason, which is the exact presentation most likely to be dismissed as flakiness and re-run -- and re-running until it passes is the laundering step that converts a real defect into an unrankable intermittency. A summary that said nothing would send the reader to the log; a summary that says zero four times tells them there is nothing to find. EVIDENCE, TWO INDEPENDENT INSTANCES IN ONE NIGHT ON TWO PULL REQUESTS WITH TWO DIFFERENT CAUSES: `required-floor: verdict=FloorRefused unexpected_failures=0 verdict_incomplete=0 non_verdict_unenrolled=0 stale_non_verdict=0` printed once above a wet-lane receipt subject-mismatch and once above a completed-over-cost refusal. Neither cause has a counter in that line; both were named on the line immediately preceding it. THE COUNTER THAT MOVED IS NOT MISSING FROM THE ARTIFACT -- IT IS IN A DIFFERENT LINE, and that is sharper than saying the cause is printed above the verdict. On a third instance the POPULATION line one row up carries `completed_over_cost_requirement=1` beside `claims_failed=0`, while the verdict line prints its own four zeros. So the verdict line is not silent and not wrong: it is a COMPLETE AND CORRECT SUMMARY OF THE WRONG POPULATION. That makes the class a SUBSET-SUMMARY rather than an omission, and it predicts the rest: ANY disposition outside the aggregation set produces the same four zeros. Two such dispositions are already known -- interrupted-before-verdict at the cpu deadline, and completed-over-cost-requirement -- so the set is under-inclusive in at least two directions rather than one, and the next arm will present identically. A RECEIPT FROM OUTSIDE CI, WHICH SHOWS THE SHAPE IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THIS ARTIFACT: a census column keyed on the interrupted arm counted 16 crossings of the cost line across 60 runs and was published; re-read over THE SAME 60 RUNS with both arms it is 22. The omitted arm was 16 runs ON ITS OWN -- the same size as the one counted, not a tail -- so a summary keyed to a proper subset of the dispositions can be wrong by ITS OWN MAGNITUDE while reading as a confident number. RECOGNITION RULE: WHEN A VERDICT IS A REFUSAL AND EVERY COUNTER BESIDE IT IS ZERO, THE COUNTER SET IS INCOMPLETE -- read it as evidence about the summary's coverage, never as evidence about the run. A partition that cannot represent the outcome that occurred is not reporting a clean run; it is reporting that it has nothing to say. THE GENERALISATION, WHICH THREE LANES REACHED SEPARATELY THE SAME NIGHT BY THREE DIFFERENT ROUTES: a key that names ONE ARM of a multi-armed disposition returns a confident, plausible, WRONG NEGATIVE for every case in the other arm. A census column keyed on the interrupted arm, a filter keyed on the deadline that raised it, and a counter keyed on one refusal class each answered cleanly and each missed the sibling arm. THE TELL IS NOT THE VALUE'S MAGNITUDE -- IT IS THAT THE KEY NAMES ONE ARM OF SOMETHING THAT HAS TWO. Ask what the OTHER arm is called before trusting a zero. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and the mitigation is that a reader happens to look one line up. CEILING: structurally impossible -- a summary derived from the SAME closed disposition coproduct the verdict is drawn from cannot omit an arm, because an unrepresented arm has no constructor. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability and not an artifact: the summary line is projected from the terminal disposition's own exhaustive match rather than assembled from a hand-chosen list of counters, so adding a refusal arm without adding its counter fails to compile. NOT satisfied by adding the two missing counters, which repairs the instances and leaves every future arm exposed in exactly the same way.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
